1. Tailoring with Münch's 4 Ps

Project in context: Maybank Secure2u

Secure2u is the safer and quick way to approve your Maybank2u and MAE transactions, using Secure Verification (one-tap approval).
This feature is available on the MAE app. Once you have activated Secure2u, it will be the default method to approve ALL transactions.

Münch's 4 Ps can be apply to prevent heavyweight process overhead from scuttling project success.
a) Problem (Complexity, novelty, volatility of constraints)

High Complexity & Novelty: 
Replacing legacy SMS TAC/MSOS codes with cryptographic device binding, push notifications, and offline time-based OTPs (Secure TAC)
integrated across Maybank2u web, MAE mobile app, and external FPX/card gateways.

High Constraint Volatility:
Rapidly evolving malware/APK scam vectors and strict, shifting anti-fraud mandates from Bank Negara Malaysia (BNM)—such as mandatory 12-hour 
cooling-off periods and ATM activation checks—requiring an adaptive process that handles sudden regulatory and security updates.

2. Project (Team size, distribution, budget, schedule)

Large, Distributed Teams: 
Requires coordinated development across multiple specialized squads (MAE mobile app teams, core banking backend, 
payment gateway/FPX teams, cybersecurity/fraud operations, and ATM firmware teams).

Strict Schedules & Enterprise Budget: 
Backed by substantial enterprise banking capital, but constrained by hard regulatory compliance deadlines from BNM (e.g., phased phase-out of SMS OTPs) 
and high coordination overhead across distributed engineering and QA units.

3. People (Skill levels, team cohesion, culture, turnover)

Specialized Skill Sets: 
Demands engineers experienced in mobile security (iOS Keychain / Android Keystore, anti-tampering/Malware Shielding), 
high-concurrency backend architecture, and regulatory compliance.

Cross-Team Cohesion & Culture: 
Bridging traditional core-banking Waterfall/governance culture with agile mobile product teams; 
standardized documentation and security handoffs are essential to mitigate knowledge loss from enterprise staff turnover.

4. Product (Criticality, reliability, lifespan, security needs)
Mission-Critical & High Reliability: 
Handles millions of daily financial authorizations where downtime directly blocks national commerce; 
requires near-zero downtime (99.99%+ availability) and strict failover mechanisms (e.g., offline Secure TAC fallback when push notifications fail).

Maximum Security & Long Lifespan: 
Because financial loss and regulatory penalties are severe, the software process cannot be lightweight—it must enforce formal threat modeling, 
penetration testing, code audits, and strict release gates over a multi-year lifecycle.

Tailoring the development process for Maybank Secure2u around Münch's 4 Ps (Problem, Project, People, Product) reveals conflicting operational demands: 
extreme safety and regulatory accountability on one side, and rapidly evolving fraud threats on the other. 
Choosing either extreme—pure ad-hoc Agile or rigid, linear Waterfall—would cause project failure.

1. Why Pure Ad-Hoc Agile Fails

Pure, unbridled Agile (minimal documentation, informal handoffs, rapid continuous deployment without heavy gates) is incompatible with Secure2u's context:

Product (Criticality & Security Needs): Secure2u authorizes millions of ringgit in daily transactions. Ad-hoc practices bypass formal threat modeling, cryptographic verification, and mandatory penetration testing, exposing customers to catastrophic financial loss and Bank Negara Malaysia (BNM) penalties.   
Project (Team Size & Distribution): Developing Secure2u spans separate MAE mobile teams, core banking ledger engineers, FPX/card gateway teams, and ATM firmware vendors. Informal, verbal Agile coordination breaks down across distributed enterprise teams, leading to integration failures and unversioned API mismatches.   
People (Turnover & Compliance Culture): In a large enterprise bank, relying on tacit knowledge rather than formal architectural specifications creates severe operational risk if key security or backend engineers leave.  

2. Why Rigid, Linear Waterfall Fails
A purely sequential, heavyweight Waterfall model (freezing all requirements upfront, multi-month phase gates before any code ships) is equally unviable:
Problem (Volatility of Constraints): Financial malware (such as malicious Android APKs intercepting notifications) and social engineering tactics evolve monthly. Under rigid Waterfall, responding to a newly discovered exploit or an urgent BNM anti-scam directive would stall in months of change-control bureaucracy while customers remain vulnerable.   
Problem (Novelty) & Product (User Friction): Features like the 12-hour cooling-off period, Malware Shielding, and ATM activation require real-world telemetry to balance security against legitimate user lockouts. Waiting until the end of a 12-month Waterfall cycle to gather user feedback would result in unusable UX or high transaction drop-off rates.  
People (Cross-Team Culture): Forcing fast-moving mobile app squads (iOS/Android) into a slow mainframe release cadence stalls UI improvements and bug fixes that do not touch core cryptographic infrastructure.

1. Scaled Agile Framework (SAFe) with Stage-Gate Governance
SAFe combines enterprise-level planning and hard milestone synchronization (prescriptive) with decentralized, sprint-based team execution (agile).
How It Works for Secure2u:
  Prescriptive Layer (Program & Portfolio Level): 
    Uses Program Increment (PI) Planning every 8–12 weeks and formal Compliance/Security Stage-Gates to align hard regulatory deadlines (e.g., Bank Negara Malaysia directives) across all dependent teams—MAE mobile squads, core banking ledger engineers, FPX gateways, and ATM firmware vendors.

  Agile Layer (Team Level): 
    Individual feature squads execute in 2-week Scrum/Kanban sprints to build UI flows, push notification handlers, and anti-malware detection updates once API contracts are locked.Alignment with Münch's 4 Ps:   Project (Team size, distribution, schedule): Directly solves multi-team coordination across large, distributed banking departments while enforcing fixed regulatory delivery dates.   

  Product (Criticality & reliability): 
    Built-in architectural runways and formal release gates ensure no feature reaches production without passing penetration testing and compliance sign-offs.   

2. Disciplined Agile (DA) — Regulated / Continuous Delivery LifecycleDisciplined 
Agile is a goal-driven, hybrid process toolkit designed specifically to tailor practices based on team context, risk, and regulatory constraints rather than forcing a single rigid methodology.
How It Works for Secure2u:
Prescriptive Layer (Inception & Transition Phases): Enforces formal upfront Architectural Modeling, threat modeling, and a dedicated Transition Phase for production readiness, security audits, and phased canary rollouts (e.g., rolling out a new Secure2u device-binding rule to 5% of users before full release).
Agile Layer (Construction Phase): Allows teams to tailor their day-to-day workflow—using iterative sprints for fast-changing mobile UX and Malware Shielding rules, while applying stricter verification loops for core cryptographic modules.
Alignment with Münch's 4 Ps:   
  Problem (Complexity & volatility of constraints): Adapts smoothly to evolving scam vectors and BNM policy changes without tearing down core security architecture.   
  People (Skill levels, culture, turnover): Balances governance artifacts (protecting against enterprise staff turnover) with embedded security specialists, avoiding the "danger of over-formalization" on low-risk UI tasks.

(Tuckman's Team Dynamics & Fist-of-Five Governance)
1. Forming

On 8 october 2026 the team was first consisting of 5 members. Quickly deciding to put the person who can be trusted with responsibility of a leader. All the
member including the elected leader agree to this decision without issue. Some ground rules were establish such as dont be afraid to ask question, no making
lone decision, do not be rude and help each other if someone is lagging behind.

2. Storming

This phase is characterised by every team member including the leader giving their opinion as to how to approach the assignment and debating each other over
how to proceed. Every team also start mentioning each other in our whatsapp group to ensure that everyone is involved in doing the work without possibility 
of a free rider.

3. Norming

After a week of casual conversation about the assignment. Cooperation improved until eventually any disagreement was solved by comparing each interpretation to the assignment 
instruction and picking the approach with the closest accuracy and become the consensus among team members. During this parts, roles were assigned to each other by each member selecting from a 
pole through first come first serve method.

4.Performing
In the last 2 week before submitting this assignment the team become one cohesive unit with a flexible work designation where each member is encourage to
supervise each others work and give comment on improvement as well as providing constructive criticism. The assignment was being develop slowly but smoothly
with little roadblock until the work is finish.


How we managed conflict during our process engineering tasks.
We managed conflict during our process engineering tasks by having a team wide discussion about the conflict, resolving it by providing evidence for each
perspective and making sure one voices does not overshadow anybody. Until the strongest evidence and all voices have been considered, the final decision is
made by picking the majority agreement.
